#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139308} :activity/id #uuid "c426ffce-64e8-55f8-994e-869d246b80bd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048285 :topic/id #uuid "9123710c-3790-5211-99a8-a771efba72bb" :topic/math-academy-id 2314} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139308 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139721 :tutorial/content "There's a way to write repeated multiplications like $2\\times 2\\times 2$ and $5\\times 5$ in a more compact way.\n\nFor the expression $2\\times 2\\times 2$, we have that $\\color{blue}2$ is multiplied by itself $\\color{red}3$ times:\n$3$\n\nTo write this more compactly, we can use the **exponent form**\n${\\color{blue}2}^{\\color{red}3}$,\npronounced as \"two to the power of three\" or \"two to the third power\".\n\nHere, the whole expression ${\\color{blue}2}^{\\color{red}3}$ is a **power**. We call $\\color{blue}2$ the **base**, and $\\color{red}3$ the **exponent**.\n\nLikewise, for the expression $5\\times 5$, we have that $\\color{blue}5$ is multiplied by itself $\\color{red}2$ times:\n$2$\n\nSo, in exponent form, the base must be $\\color{blue}5$ and the exponent must be ${\\color{red}2}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}5} \\times {\\color{blue}5} = {\\color{blue}5}^{\\color{red}2}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c176b98a-0688-507f-8d77-71a81e5143b9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8805 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e18412b2-3ef3-50b3-be9e-dd408a4b5d48" :step/math-academy-id 174280 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139309}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139309 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138397 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138174 :question/id #uuid "ebca2372-6332-546a-865a-b1389c08dd49" :question/math-academy-id "e-14180"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b74abc9-9563-5e6e-bdae-3371ff742ba9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138756}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing Repeated Multiplication of One-Digit Numbers Using Exponents"} :step/id #uuid "8bd1565f-3e8c-584c-a08f-41809affd779" :step/math-academy-id 174281 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139310}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139310 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138398 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138175 :question/id #uuid "cd28f5fb-117d-5290-b89e-78d59d73cf58" :question/math-academy-id "e-14181"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d063a5-e148-544b-a42a-7d8a1ee10c90" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138844}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing Repeated Multiplication of Larger Numbers Using Exponents"} :step/id #uuid "4793316e-9fe1-5c4b-af01-cd7c40ce97d0" :step/math-academy-id 174284 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139311}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139311 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138399 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138176 :question/id #uuid "4d103ec6-54ce-5bee-b1f4-9f36f468cddc" :question/math-academy-id "e-14183"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f80c4a5c-c03a-5b5a-afab-d39abc720598" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138842}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing Exponents as Repeated Multiplication"} :step/id #uuid "b6814b2b-a7d5-52d5-b61e-2731ea15ee18" :step/math-academy-id 174287 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139312}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139312 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138400 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138177 :question/id #uuid "10c85ed5-dea4-53d9-8d5a-5588c724de5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14185"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a49001aa-bf42-5005-9bf0-940caab49b8f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138845}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing Larger Exponents as Repeated Multiplication"} :step/id #uuid "32b30ceb-6a06-5d05-9dcc-a6e9b134a8c4" :step/math-academy-id 174290}] :activity/title "Introduction to Exponents" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138618}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139583} :activity/id #uuid "958bf76d-900f-5728-a665-cbbaccc0d8c0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048566 :topic/id #uuid "aa5df21b-533b-51c0-b02d-90918cede430" :topic/math-academy-id 2356} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139583 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139703 :tutorial/content "Often, we can subtract mixed numbers in a similar way to adding them. Let's remind ourselves of the steps:\n\n> **Step 1**: We subtract whole numbers from whole numbers,\n> **Step 2**: Then, we subtract fractions from fractions, converting to equivalent fractions with a common denominator when necessary.\n> **Step 3**: We combine the results from steps 1 and 2.\n\nLet's use this method to find the value of\n\n$$\n3\\,\\dfrac{3}{4} - 2\\,\\dfrac 1 8\n$$\n\n**Step 1**: Subtracting the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n3-2 = {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Now, we subtract the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} - \\dfrac 1 8\n$$\n\nHere, we can make a common denominator of $8$. To put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 4\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $8$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{3\\times 2}{4\\times 2} = \\dfrac{6}{8}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same and we subtract the numerators.\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{8} - \\frac{1}{8} = \\frac{5}{8}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We now combine our results to get the final answer:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}1} + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 8}} = {\\color{red}{1}}\\,{\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 8}}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f94a7ba4-c6be-565c-aec5-11441b7bef13" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8593 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "254938fe-8ac6-59c7-a5f1-479e3b277348" :step/math-academy-id 96487 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139584}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138575 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138139 :question/id #uuid "8f412bc3-e3fb-56da-8ded-e201ca5086c5" :question/math-academy-id "e-13767"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a938039c-93b3-59e8-b917-ad1b9aa8f2f9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050637}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139072}] :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting Two Mixed Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "bc7e9ba9-e6f1-5a37-b839-e89ad252c700" :step/math-academy-id 96488 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139585}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139585 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138576 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138140 :question/id #uuid "6227ddee-c908-57ca-aaaf-c39fe162b4db" :question/math-academy-id "e-13769"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58ea7610-365c-5fee-8e8f-3d6fd68cd196" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139181}] :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Whole Number Part Equals Zero"} :step/id #uuid "e58386f1-1d63-53dd-b912-2fdacf8eab19" :step/math-academy-id 96491 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139586}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139586 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138577 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138141 :question/id #uuid "0f46d710-abf6-5fb0-a305-9b2a023555d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-13770"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c87e441-a534-5f6b-82e7-34e2ef8b9639" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050444}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139073}] :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting Two Mixed Numbers Using the Least Common Denominator"} :step/id #uuid "dc1f6285-6037-5159-bf65-12e7d5ce6582" :step/math-academy-id 96494}] :activity/title "Subtracting Mixed Numbers With Unlike Denominators" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138656}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143611} :activity/id #uuid "365178a6-d3ea-574a-b6d0-a6068664280d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048602 :topic/id #uuid "add94d79-c4d1-5676-88a9-a21eff5b5716" :topic/math-academy-id 2440} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144037 :tutorial/content "The **least common multiple** of two numbers is the smallest value that is a multiple of both the numbers.\n\nTo illustrate, let's find the least common multiple of $3$ and $5$.\n\nWe start by listing the multiples of each number. Then we find the smallest number that appears in both lists.\n\n- Multiples of $3$: $\\quad 3, 6, 9, 12, {\\color{blue}15}, \\ldots$\n- Multiples of $5$: $\\quad 5, 10, {\\color{blue}15}, 20, \\ldots$\n\nWe conclude that the least common multiple of $3$ and $5$ is ${\\color{blue}{15}}$.\n\nWe can visualize the least common multiple of $3$ and $5$ using a diagram like the one below.\n\n![](images/67/67254f47d80d6c319166affbf77c083b3b9e1c8c9ce79d9ee6016772c139ad69.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "3b006bb6-a2b9-5b46-bb2d-9cc360ae0ca4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8423 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "edbef501-6a7d-5ae8-91c3-3807bb4d93bf" :step/math-academy-id 128972 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143612}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142758 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142528 :question/id #uuid "67c29e35-a48b-54a3-b4e4-7109b6381af1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13454"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eaec1da3-c8c9-56e5-9331-c467fb5ad2d0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050212}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143520}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least Common Multiple of Two One-Digit Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "bffb1f4e-4feb-5220-bd91-cbeac26ce9c4" :step/math-academy-id 128973 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142759 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142529 :question/id #uuid "dd4cce99-a9a4-502d-8aff-c363c318cd23" :question/math-academy-id "e-13456"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fddffcd-a83a-59c6-b406-cd427f5a525c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143514}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least Common Multiple of a One-Digit and Two-Digit Number"} :step/id #uuid "9110ebdc-5cf2-5daf-b82f-6e7e79794efd" :step/math-academy-id 128976 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143614}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142760 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142530 :question/id #uuid "9fb3d065-77aa-50df-95fc-f061f1f4cfbd" :question/math-academy-id "e-13458"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b147195-8026-5716-acd6-f3a119706101" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143515}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least Common Multiple of Two Numbers: Word Problems"} :step/id #uuid "d5fd7fd8-182d-509f-a1f2-25016f33160d" :step/math-academy-id 128980 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143615}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142761 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142531 :question/id #uuid "d3bfca22-96bd-5f03-97c9-b33318abaa76" :question/math-academy-id "e-13460"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03ba798b-dc64-5b63-86e6-1973b38f11bd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143178}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least Common Multiple of Three Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "63f67e04-de65-5752-9a08-d516c416eb27" :step/math-academy-id 128983}] :activity/title "Least Common Multiples" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186143066}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074596} :activity/id #uuid "d3604c58-3245-50b4-807b-f7e5bcdb7911" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048907 :topic/id #uuid "cb71b46a-ae82-5065-bf55-e0638d1e7401" :topic/math-academy-id 543} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075059 :tutorial/content "We can use fraction models to help us to add fractions and whole numbers.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find the value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2 + 1\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction. Let's solve this problem using a fraction model.\n\n![](images/5c/5c1cd8994310039da418bd8983b9019c483931faca9fd6e49082f670f8a56f38.png)\n\nWe cannot add these two fractions at the moment, because they are split into different numbers of parts (i.e., they have different denominators). So what do we do?\n\nThe first shape is split into $\\color{red}2$ equal parts. So, we can split the whole number into $\\color{red}2$ equal parts also:\n\n![](images/08/086383f205c2725283ab00901e68681ec1b3e55aea2ae48639ec85519732c1ef.png)\n\nNow, both shapes are split into $\\color{red}2$ equal parts, and we have\n\n$$\n1+2 = {\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\nshaded parts in total. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2 + 1 = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}}{{\\color{red}2}}\n$$\n\nAnd that's our answer!\n\nLearning to add fractions and whole numbers will give us vital clues on how to add fractions with *unlike* denominators, which is our ultimate goal." :tutorial/id #uuid "41682d46-db1d-506e-9bec-f61e2f4f67a3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8985 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "00f4a492-92fe-565a-9909-335acbedb5d5" :step/math-academy-id 139261 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074597}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074597 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073710 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073504 :question/id #uuid "3d1dc1f5-c9d6-5bbc-8dad-e50b6a3a0837" :question/math-academy-id "e-14535"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e14ef759-e8f0-5e99-9f3c-d8678273655d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051077}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074347}] :knowledge-point/title "Adding Fractions and Whole Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "b59d8e94-b251-5c67-afd6-16b99abd6971" :step/math-academy-id 139262 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074598}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074598 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073711 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073505 :question/id #uuid "4e2f0150-b229-54ae-80f1-933807b51228" :question/math-academy-id "e-14538"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c16e795d-8a5c-58b5-aa42-f185ebe68c9d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074348}] :knowledge-point/title "Splitting Whole Numbers Into Parts"} :step/id #uuid "7017cb5a-3a99-5caf-a76e-6a4e616c3c8b" :step/math-academy-id 139265 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074599}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074599 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073712 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073506 :question/id #uuid "1bf7aca9-8db8-551d-98e6-d57cb789ed61" :question/math-academy-id "e-14541"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15b6296b-11a1-53da-a243-aacc734e82cb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074352}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Addition Problems Using Fraction Models"} :step/id #uuid "4fcdc8a5-7906-5504-9559-3ef0119e852d" :step/math-academy-id 139268}] :activity/title "Adding Fractions and Whole Numbers Using Models" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073963}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065131} :activity/id #uuid "af1ad9c9-eddf-59bb-aedf-24a165cae134" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048911 :topic/id #uuid "cbbdf6cb-f6ca-5deb-b060-a316432db0ee" :topic/math-academy-id 327} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065131 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065412 :tutorial/content "When we subtract a positive number from another positive number, we move towards the left on the number line.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following subtraction problem:\n\n$$\n3 - 5\n$$\n\nTo subtract $5$ from $3$, we start at $3$ on the number line and move $5$ places to the left.\n\n![](images/57/57ac1fe7a28ae5fefadf900d2276d499cdccf01df48b819b0aafa5347a2ec506.png)\n\nWhen we do this, we land at $-2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n3-5 = -2\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8af27dde-6aa4-5067-84f8-9538f1acaa18" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8029 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e99d8d22-f103-5560-a608-35f0957133f4" :step/math-academy-id 188374 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065132}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065132 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064255 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063935 :question/id #uuid "34280e7c-023c-5c7f-b22b-3f322ff97dad" :question/math-academy-id "e-12813"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a3c970b-4086-52ef-ac0f-2b8c3d72fd51" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049429}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064629}] :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting a Positive Number From a Non-Negative Number"} :step/id #uuid "a7e8a41f-1f30-5c54-b22b-c51e7a5efd82" :step/math-academy-id 188375 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065133}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065133 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065413 :tutorial/content "It is impractical to use number lines to subtract large numbers.\n\nHowever, a neat trick makes subtracting a positive number from a smaller positive number very easy!\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following subtraction problem:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{37}} - {\\color{blue}{68}}\n$$\n\nSince $68$ is larger than $37$, the answer *must be negative*.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem: subtracting the numbers *in the reverse order*. In our case, the reverse problem is\n${\\color{blue}{68}} - {\\color{red}{37}}$.\n\nNext, we carry out the subtraction:\n\n$$\n6\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}1\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{68}} - {\\color{red}{37}} = 31\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{37}} - {\\color{blue}{68}} = -31\n$$\n\nAnd that's it!\n\nLet's use the same idea to subtract two three-digit numbers." :tutorial/id #uuid "d7dbb60e-47d5-56db-981a-145c532f7ace" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8030 :tutorial/title "Subtraction With Larger Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "12b96179-bf32-5136-bbf1-e7631a656dfb" :step/math-academy-id 188379 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065134}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065134 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064256 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063936 :question/id #uuid "1850f823-4159-5bd1-afac-71d8090c754b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12814"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1c51da0-7569-50c0-b405-c74419d47ef3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049516}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064528}] :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting a Positive Number From a Non-Negative Number With Larger Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "9f2475d1-01ac-524a-9a5e-a1adfdc797a2" :step/math-academy-id 188380}] :activity/title "Subtracting a Positive Number From a Smaller Positive Number" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064418}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158165} :activity/id #uuid "edd51738-a7e7-5119-a156-2948a25e0822" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048967 :topic/id #uuid "cfefea77-2afb-561b-ba1b-49d09f5abb4f" :topic/math-academy-id 2805} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158036 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158604 :tutorial/content "A number is divisible by $4$ if the *last two digits* are divisible by $4$.\n\nLet's determine whether the following number is divisible by $4{:}$\n\n$$\n124, 424, 6{\\color{blue}{24}}\n$$\n\nThe number formed by the last two digits is ${\\color{blue}{24}}$, and ${\\color{blue}{24}}$ is divisible by $4$. Therefore, $124, 424, 624$ is divisible by $4$.\n\nNow, consider the following number:\n\n$$\n251, 8{\\color{red}{18}}\n$$\n\nThe number formed by the last two digits is ${\\color{red}{18}}$, and ${\\color{red}{18}}$ is *not* divisible by $4$. Therefore, $251, 818$ is *not* divisible by $4$.\n\nWe sometimes need to use long division to determine whether the last two digits are divisible by $4$.\n\nFor example, the number\n$1,7{\\color{blue}{84}}$\nis divisible by $4$ because ${\\color{blue}{84}}$ is divisible by $4$. We can check this using long division.\n\n| $2$$1$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$0$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$0$$4$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nFinally, note that if a number is divisible by $4$, then it is divisible by $2$. Consequently, any number whose last digit is odd is *not* divisible by $4$." :tutorial/id #uuid "91876d6f-1b93-5d6d-adf9-a0af7e58aca7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9438 :tutorial/title "Divisibility by 4"} :step/id #uuid "bc976df9-d5c5-512d-a6c4-5b7fa78feb28" :step/math-academy-id 172421 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158154}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158153 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157306 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157110 :question/id #uuid "f60fce07-0a28-5893-8918-157241667e26" :question/math-academy-id "e-7488"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41a2fa93-ff99-507f-98ca-3db7347f843f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157858}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Number is Divisible by 2, 5, or 10"} :step/id #uuid "3d87beb1-3c40-501d-9b47-96a06e457197" :step/math-academy-id 27826 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158036}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158154 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157307 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157111 :question/id #uuid "c85fb0bd-0120-5b4a-871e-79ca75a363dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-7489"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a25bce44-98ca-54c6-a59f-b7508880d5ba" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048145}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157850}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Number is Divisible by 4"} :step/id #uuid "4e56c82e-779e-500a-86ec-09a1f92663f3" :step/math-academy-id 27827 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158173}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158165 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158490 :tutorial/content "Dividing very large numbers can take some effort. Thankfully, there are some rules that allow us to determine whether one number is divisible by another *without* having to carry out the division!\n\nA number is divisible by $2$ if the *last digit* is an even number. In other words, if the last digit is ${\\color{blue}{0}},{\\color{blue}{2}},{\\color{blue}{4}},{\\color{blue}{6}}$, or ${\\color{blue}{8}}$, then the number is divisible by $2$.\n\nFor example, the following numbers are divisible by $2$ because their last digit is even.\n$51{\\color{blue}{0}}, \\qquad 785{\\color{blue}{2}}, \\qquad 232,65{\\color{blue}{4}}, \\qquad 197,478,24{\\color{blue}{6}}, \\qquad 578,197,478,24{\\color{blue}{8}}$\n\nOn the other hand, the following numbers are *not* divisible by $2$ because their last digit is *not* even.\n$35{\\color{red}{1}}, \\qquad 547{\\color{red}{3}}, \\qquad 58,68{\\color{red}{5}}, \\qquad 239,63{\\color{red}{7}}, \\qquad 100,000,000,00{\\color{red}{9}}$\n\nWe also have some easy divisibility rules for $5$ and $10$. Again, they depend only on the last digit:\n\n- A number is divisible by $5$ if the *last digit* is $5$ or $0$.\n- A number is divisible by $10$ if the *last digit* is $0$.\n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "f79c4bec-8582-50b0-b80c-7a153bd0fbf3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4524 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "545a5913-05ca-53cd-ba7f-28108c16e428" :step/math-academy-id 27848 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158153}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158166 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157318 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157122 :question/id #uuid "ffcf03fc-edc1-5d63-9346-1bf60d162317" :question/math-academy-id "e-7504"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50cd6e99-6275-5a6a-9c91-d316392ec8ca" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049143}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157852}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Number is Divisible by 8"} :step/id #uuid "e9dbfbbc-e3ad-5ff2-b1bf-89b8dc9c4147" :step/math-academy-id 27849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158173 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158496 :tutorial/content "A number is divisible by $8$ if the last three digits form a number that is divisible by $8$.\n\nFor example, consider the number $845\\,128\\,016$. The last three digits form the number $016 = 16$, which is divisible by $8$. Therefore, the number $845\\,128\\,016$ is divisible by $8$.\n\nOkay, that was easy. But what about the number $745\\,254\\,786\\,768$? Is this divisible by $8$?\n\nThe last three digits form the number $768$. Dividing $768$ by $8$ requires a bit more work. However, there is an easy rule to check whether a $3$-digit number is divisible by $8$. That is, we add four times the hundreds digit to twice the tens digit and the ones digit, and then check if the result is divisible by $8$. In our case, we have\n\n$$\n4(7) + 2(6) + 8 = 28+12+8 = 48\n$$\n\nwhich is divisible by $8$. Therefore, $745\\,254\\,786\\,768$ is divisible by $8$.\n\nFinally, note that if a number is not divisible by $2$ *and* $4$, then it cannot be divisible by $8$. Sometimes, it's easiest to check those first." :tutorial/id #uuid "b5e0fe81-8ae3-5806-b8cf-3039fdc050a1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4570 :tutorial/title "Divisibility by 8"} :step/id #uuid "6db52270-24c1-5608-a3a7-fd26378cec20" :step/math-academy-id 28079 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158166}}] :activity/title "Divisibility of Integers by 2, 5, 10, 4, and 8" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157469}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074886} :activity/id #uuid "d20c5f62-a906-5fd3-838a-0e61904c6091" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049010 :topic/id #uuid "d328b161-bbaf-5ed8-bebd-8cc56a233314" :topic/math-academy-id 562} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074886 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074972 :tutorial/content "A **percentage** is another way of expressing a fraction that's used often in everyday situations. The word **percent** means \"out of $100$.\"\n\nLet's take a look at the square below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c2fd8e09cd4c0e7d6160c96c29b644c22e17c09d80e025e3cced2b391a20c94.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- the square has been divided into $100$ equal parts, and\n- $\\color{blue}50$ of those parts are shaded.\n\nSince $\\color{blue}50$ out of $100$ parts are shaded, we say that \"$\\color{blue}50$ percent of the square is shaded.\"\n\nWe have a special symbol that we use instead of the word percent. This symbol is written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Large{\\%}\n$$\n\nUsing this symbol, we say that \"${\\color{blue}{5}0}\\%$ of the square is shaded.\"" :tutorial/id #uuid "1394fc74-71ad-5f1c-b032-940908e96849" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2071 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "08ccc6b4-9e8e-58ae-8c72-9c8924f4ac4f" :step/math-academy-id 7928 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074887}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074887 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073890 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073576 :question/id #uuid "77ca02d3-234c-512d-bd8f-7a8d3ca111f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-2308"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a14c0c3-9351-5e52-8cd1-c79dd13d19b9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049517}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074474}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Percents Less Than 100% Using 100-Piece Models"} :step/id #uuid "b9ebb937-0b48-58ea-b518-557295155139" :step/math-academy-id 7929 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074888}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074888 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074973 :tutorial/content "When more than $100$ pieces are shaded in a model where $100$ pieces represent a whole, the percentage will be greater than $100\\%$.\n\nLet's take a look at the model below, where one large square represents one whole:\n\n![](images/5f/5fcb68226ada21bec7e2f4dd90fd5c9fde2c43578e1611e0faf85ddb580b5f34.png)\n\nIn this model, $170$ tiles are shaded. Therefore, this model represents $170\\%$." :tutorial/id #uuid "e1f95e0f-2d10-50e4-9cd1-a76c2fc35836" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2073 :tutorial/title "Percentages Greater Than 100%"} :step/id #uuid "a54d3ef4-500e-59c3-ae05-4b3a53133012" :step/math-academy-id 7935 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074889}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074889 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073891 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073577 :question/id #uuid "4d698fe6-f5af-5915-8b63-152d67c52774" :question/math-academy-id "e-2312"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6df29c3-7a13-5c93-b14d-70064f993462" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051077}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074479}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Percentages Greater Than 100% Using 100-Piece Models"} :step/id #uuid "0f0f170d-881d-5fd9-9bd7-874a2f2c72ec" :step/math-academy-id 7942 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074890}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074890 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074974 :tutorial/content "How do we find the percentage represented by a fraction model when the model isn't split into $100$ equal pieces?\n\nThe answer is to express the fraction as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $100$.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the model below:\n\n![](images/17/17f55289981c45c7473462f06592a9577d692a9efba462b1101022ae8a630a4f.png)\n\nThis model represents the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nTo make an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $100$, we multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $20$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5} = \\dfrac{4 \\times 20}{5 \\times 20} = \\dfrac{80}{100}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{80}{100}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $80\\%$, we conclude that our fraction model represents $80\\%$.\n\nWe can also use equivalent fractions to construct a simplified fraction model for a given percent. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "f7140f82-2538-52a9-88ea-c84935add135" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2077 :tutorial/title "Representing Percentages Using Other Models"} :step/id #uuid "b3811aed-919d-5324-a2b3-37a2fc60a086" :step/math-academy-id 7948 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074891}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074891 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073892 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073578 :question/id #uuid "62f2309e-804b-5ccc-9dbc-c8c6cc1a04b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-2321"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3f6dc98-c317-50ca-adc7-8f0b0b79d407" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050735}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074482}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Percentages Less Than 100% Using Other Models"} :step/id #uuid "bc4b3a3b-3e46-5cab-95e5-4fe9b7a9b2d6" :step/math-academy-id 7949 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074892}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074892 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073893 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073579 :question/id #uuid "152c00bd-bae3-5abf-b461-0c031b4fb462" :question/math-academy-id "e-2322"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "477ee69a-937b-54cb-a2e7-4dc6f023f0bd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050249}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074481}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Percentages Greater Than 100% Using Other Models"} :step/id #uuid "efe69386-b7fd-59d6-9a72-33a398873396" :step/math-academy-id 7955}] :activity/title "Understanding Percentages Using Models" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073980}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065293} :activity/id #uuid "87fa48fe-260e-5396-9f68-9ac420c9528d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049349 :topic/id #uuid "ede67d03-aa98-5b07-b1a1-e05056a85575" :topic/math-academy-id 298} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065293 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065415 :tutorial/content "Multiplying with negative numbers can be summed up by two simple rules.\n\nThe first rule says that when we multiply a negative number by a positive number, the result (**product**) is a negative number. We can picture this multiplication rule as\n\n$$\n(-)\\cdot(+) = (-)or(+)\\cdot(-) = (-)\n$$\n\nFor instance, let's find out the value of $(-3) \\cdot 10$.\nHere, we have a product of a negative number $\\color{red}(-3)$ and a positive one ${\\color{blue}(10)}$. So the product will be negative:\n$30$\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we multiply $3$ by $10$ without any signs:\n\n$$\n3 \\cdot 10 = 30\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-3)} \\cdot {\\color{blue}10} = {\\color{red} -30}\n$$\n\nNote that multiplication is **commutative**, meaning it doesn't matter in what order we multiply two numbers. Commutativity means that we're allowed to swap the numbers, which gives\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-3)} \\cdot {\\color{blue}10} = {\\color{blue}10}\\cdot {\\color{red}(-3)} = {\\color{red} -30}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1eb02261-90e4-51bc-a24e-dabdedaafa63" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8105 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "480bb98f-29a4-57e1-9342-972680f415f8" :step/math-academy-id 82238 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065294}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064363 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063939 :question/id #uuid "9e3473f6-80c1-5559-b29f-ac0bcb2f3a13" :question/math-academy-id "e-12967"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75901336-1d0e-51f1-acde-d6eb784d9f82" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049429}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064576}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Positive Number by a Negative Number"} :step/id #uuid "cb9bb839-e0dc-5aca-9cc9-ae22d07a3486" :step/math-academy-id 82239 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065295}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064364 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063940 :question/id #uuid "e4e645fe-d1cd-584c-b6a3-4e75e1170a34" :question/math-academy-id "e-12969"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cb97145-a93a-5df3-8dbe-6777d21601c8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051030}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064974}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Positive Number by a Negative Number With Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "b387ed41-0522-5959-a882-fcd13a445bf5" :step/math-academy-id 82242}] :activity/title "Multiplying Positive Numbers With Negative Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064401}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106957} :activity/id #uuid "bd3c7493-58ee-57a8-8d3a-3cd07eb7026e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049350 :topic/id #uuid "edfb49a6-b5b4-5d01-815a-215980c21139" :topic/math-academy-id 1378} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106833 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105858 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105436 :question/id #uuid "a9ca2de5-c0ad-5bba-a432-0e62fdcd7d9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10583"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9e4943a-72d7-545e-b746-3a19ee6d3a83" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106051}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Segments and Their Notation"} :step/id #uuid "3a8029d6-f569-5e02-94f4-a8c43054eba8" :step/math-academy-id 40003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106834 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105859 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105437 :question/id #uuid "acae6ded-25d0-5ed3-bd39-8c81ecd9a4d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-10603"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f50316d-ff00-53a1-af3a-fedd68e91f43" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106050}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rays and Their Notation"} :step/id #uuid "79e005c1-44a4-5a62-80df-af13329c4744" :step/math-academy-id 40094 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106836}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106835 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107135 :tutorial/content "A **ray** is similar to a line but has a starting point and extends infinitely in one direction only. An example of a ray is shown below.\n\n![](images/75/750aef546281b34fef8e9aef61c1260894128138022e971d6c19dfd834cfa1e2.png)\n\nA ray that starts at the point $P$ and passes through a point $Q$ is expressed as\n$\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{PQ}$.\n\nWe use the \"$\\longrightarrow$\" symbol to denote that the line extends forever in **one** direction. Notice that the arrow's tail is above the starting point $P$.\n\nSimilar to lines, rays have no width but are infinite in length." :tutorial/id #uuid "74865d26-a257-5d2d-a7cd-537189af9954" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6647 :tutorial/title "Rays"} :step/id #uuid "0bee6a5d-8341-54c8-b8a6-8740368cff99" :step/math-academy-id 40242 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106834}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106836 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107136 :tutorial/content "A **segment** is part of a line bounded by two points, called the **endpoints** of the segment. An example of a segment is shown below.\n\n![](images/6f/6f0d8c12318cc57412be73f2cfb077efa5b06472554e75be41af233c87a9ab17.png)\n\nA segment with endpoints $A$ and $B$ is denoted $\\overline{AB}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "ee97c2fb-0c77-5af6-b979-587c04a1f0c9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6648 :tutorial/title "Segments"} :step/id #uuid "4f3e9956-cbc9-58f4-8ef4-625ad217137b" :step/math-academy-id 40243 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106833}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106957 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107041 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll discuss some fundamental objects in geometry. The objects we'll discuss are some of the building blocks we use to describe all geometrical objects.\n\nA **point** is an exact location in space. It has no size (i.e., no length, width, or depth), only position. A point is indicated with a dot usually labeled with a capital letter (e.g., $P$, $Q$, $S$,...), as shown below.\n\n![](images/ac/aceb9052aee93f2d7ef5cfc3f6a9c7ace291e6c9525751a2d826c0fbfa0b36ed.png)\n\nA **line** is a straight object that is infinitely long and has no width. We can think of it as an infinite collection of points, extending infinitely in both directions. A line has no endpoints!\n\nLet's take a look at the following line.\n\n![](images/86/861fbcc84e4e293379616a5e18d9073ecfdf4af5f6d17dece22196d332b62899.png)\n\nA line that passes through the points $P$ and $Q$ is expressed as\n$\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ}$.\n\nWe use the \"$\\longleftrightarrow$\" symbol to denote that the line extends forever in **both** directions.\n\nLines can also be indicated with lowercase letters. For example:\n\n![](images/0a/0a2d566ad6126527f4b1a2b5a992688a668b9ff901c6b7716381521a576a0594.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "a6361854-d380-59ab-8f5c-a64142f383c8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2161 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5170c6de-909a-58ce-863d-4886baeefb53" :step/math-academy-id 8689 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106958}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106958 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105925 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105520 :question/id #uuid "569e004c-b3c0-5389-9942-fd6dbcad046e" :question/math-academy-id "e-2540"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8db8b0ae-1a2a-546a-884d-bdf773c75483" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106528}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Lines and Their Notation"} :step/id #uuid "8ecf8f9e-5978-5c97-8a02-ed6688aea1aa" :step/math-academy-id 8690 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106835}}] :activity/title "Points, Lines, Rays, and Segments" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106005}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143728} :activity/id #uuid "91dfe0ab-ab42-5501-85e0-b0f434330678" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049356 :topic/id #uuid "ee4b1f44-89b8-542f-9f2c-2243b6b05c5b" :topic/math-academy-id 2388} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143728 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144074 :tutorial/content "The **reciprocal** of a fraction is a bit like turning it upside down: we switch the numerator and denominator.\n\nTo find the reciprocal of a\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{20}\n$$\n\nwe switch the numerator and the denominator as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\color{red}20} \\quad\\rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}20}{\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\nNow we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20} 1 = 20\n$$\n\nSo the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{20}\n$$\n\nis $20$." :tutorial/id #uuid "db280674-4a34-5dbd-99a3-c1c2c0ecf8be" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8576 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b9634965-7a0f-50bf-bc7a-b2626cc77c74" :step/math-academy-id 174394 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143729}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143729 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142837 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142599 :question/id #uuid "0028c4d7-e672-5dd7-b951-3966be4266a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13731"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "257a88af-0849-5cf9-b74a-2b2694ee7e6c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143300}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reciprocal of a Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "86003208-694a-59f7-a78d-1d9cef22ac2b" :step/math-academy-id 174395 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143730}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143730 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142838 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142600 :question/id #uuid "bf0771a2-9eaa-5ad9-911e-18f529edcd35" :question/math-academy-id "e-13734"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3ab73e9-c0fe-596c-90c6-9fede336df7a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050249}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143319}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Reciprocal of a Fraction as a Mixed Number"} :step/id #uuid "00476cba-2f5d-5364-9604-f4ca1d6c706b" :step/math-academy-id 174398 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143731}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143731 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144075 :tutorial/content "To find the reciprocal of a whole number, we must first write the number as a fraction.\n\nFor instance, let's write the number $2$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n2= \\dfrac{2} 1\n$$\n\nTo find the reciprocal of a fraction, we switch the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}1} \\quad\\rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nSo the reciprocal of $2$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "439c60e5-dcf3-50b0-bc8f-a2d2ce74cdcc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8577 :tutorial/title "The Reciprocal of a Whole Number"} :step/id #uuid "826383f3-7e5a-57f1-a9a9-8d7e2104cdbf" :step/math-academy-id 174401 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143732}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143732 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142839 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142601 :question/id #uuid "769b40a0-d0c9-58c1-b317-a80cb4b6a4bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-13736"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4ec98e7-8bf6-53da-89fc-a816c495ee31" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143315}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reciprocal of a Whole Number"} :step/id #uuid "9043ac43-54a0-55ac-af0b-b8209415b37e" :step/math-academy-id 174402 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143733}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143733 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144076 :tutorial/content "To find the reciprocal of a mixed number, we must first convert it to an improper fraction.\n\nLet's find the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we convert the given mixed number to an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{(1\\times 3)+2}{3}=\\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nTo find the reciprocal of a fraction, we switch the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}5}{\\color{red}3} \\quad\\rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nSo the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ae66510a-0eed-5ee9-8a6e-20d52291d930" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8578 :tutorial/title "The Reciprocal of a Mixed Number"} :step/id #uuid "cc73d26f-4867-5213-9687-4bb629751e8f" :step/math-academy-id 174405 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143734}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143734 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142840 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142602 :question/id #uuid "9fc449fc-a8f9-5e70-b670-b28eab0ef4c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-13737"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db24977e-2538-5cd2-8c31-2e8f0e8ac134" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049316}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143323}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reciprocal of a Mixed Number"} :step/id #uuid "a54e63c9-f396-57e3-973b-4cb92a8cc901" :step/math-academy-id 174406}] :activity/title "Reciprocals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186143018}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148482} :activity/id #uuid "e30f8e58-bf46-5a3b-b6e5-78f40e3c48c1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049531 :topic/id #uuid "fc9ea30d-c77e-520f-8dab-e177eee4a483" :topic/math-academy-id 2457} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148482 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148698 :tutorial/content "When working with fractions, percents, and decimals, we often need to decide which number is greater or smaller. Since these numbers are written in different forms, it can be difficult to compare them directly.\n\nTo make comparisons easier, we can *convert them to a common form*.\n\nFor example, let's compare the percent $48\\%$ and the decimal $0.52$.\n\nOne way to do that is to convert the percent to a decimal. So, we divide the percent value by $100{:}$\n\n$$\n48 \\div 100 = 0.48\n$$\n\nThus, $48\\%$ is equivalent to $0.48$.\n\nNow, we can compare the two decimals:\n$0.48 < 0.52$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that $48\\%$ is smaller than $0.52$.\n\nLet's see more examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "e6681afa-a432-5074-a46c-204561f2cf65" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2078 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d9c7592c-10c0-5cc3-b1ff-b5b67dfe08ba" :step/math-academy-id 632742 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148483}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147542 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147245 :question/id #uuid "7dd3b106-ac45-522a-97ea-6dc4355f0247" :question/math-academy-id "e-2317"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6be40082-db16-5983-aa43-9b6ee8f59def" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050030}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147796}] :knowledge-point/title "Comparing a Decimal to a Percent"} :step/id #uuid "5df02fb6-3576-5996-8b1e-64978fa7b5d8" :step/math-academy-id 632743 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148484}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148484 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147543 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147246 :question/id #uuid "22bfefb3-89b4-5d42-b305-732790c89fbd" :question/math-academy-id "e-2318"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37060efd-560b-5d57-b108-07c202e3abcc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050478}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147798}] :knowledge-point/title "Comparing a Percent to a Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "f6835341-5e27-57e3-ae67-a97b6df1ba75" :step/math-academy-id 632746 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148485}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148485 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148670 :tutorial/content "Suppose Ethan and Ryan are baseball players. In the previous season, $24\\%$ of Ethan’s hits were home runs, while the decimal proportion of Ryan’s hits that were home runs was $0.3$.\n\nLet’s determine whether either Ethan or Ryan hit home runs on more than half of their total hits.\n\n- *For Ethan*, we compare his statistic to one-half written as a percentage, $50\\%$. Since\n$24\\% < 50\\%$,\nless than half of Ethan’s hits were home runs.\n- *For Ryan*, we compare his statistic to one-half written as a decimal, $0.5$. Since\n$0.3 < 0.5$,\nless than half of Ryan’s hits were home runs.\n\nTherefore, neither player hit home runs on more than half of their hits.\n\nNow, let’s see more concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "fbb4d977-7db5-579e-baf0-bb8e13b6c15a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14935 :tutorial/title "Comparing Decimals and Percents in Context"} :step/id #uuid "4ce04142-5c86-5b4c-a76f-55af88901484" :step/math-academy-id 632749 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148486}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148486 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147544 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147235 :question/id #uuid "4016fd3c-d8b2-5ef2-8554-47ac5c183950" :question/math-academy-id "e-22017"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "638e604b-5416-5d3c-a61f-5be645aa255b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147802}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements in Context"} :step/id #uuid "c0e51654-69d7-5097-b27f-71f3c28bce97" :step/math-academy-id 632750 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148487}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148671 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose Carlos and Henry have the same number of trading cards. A total of $32 \\%$ of Carlos's cards are rare cards, while\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nof Henry's cards are rare cards.\n\nWho has more rare cards?\n\nTo answer this, we need to *compare the fraction and the percentage*:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 32 \\%\n$$\n\nA common method is to convert the fraction to a percentage.\n\nFirst, we write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nas an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{1 \\times 25}{4 \\times 25} = \\dfrac{25}{100}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{25}{100} = 25 \\%\n$$\n\nSince $25 \\% < 32 \\%$, we can conclude that Carlos has *more* rare cards than Carlos." :tutorial/id #uuid "4be68676-8fcc-56ce-a8ef-96b2a954a03c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14937 :tutorial/title "Comparing Fractions and Percents in Context"} :step/id #uuid "99330381-c770-5a62-a3c7-a79ba13fcd0b" :step/math-academy-id 632753 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148488}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147545 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147247 :question/id #uuid "e6b550b1-cd66-5a70-9d11-d37733ac4c5f" :question/math-academy-id "e-2319"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d39373d-ba41-53aa-8119-4ecf5138a50a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147800}] :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Fractions and Percents: Word Problem"} :step/id #uuid "5102e680-a474-5349-a6c9-27bc67303b7a" :step/math-academy-id 632754}] :activity/title "Comparing Fractions, Percents, and Decimals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147658}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139533} :activity/id #uuid "f6fac74e-e2d7-5db4-a32b-99183dac9daf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049688 :topic/id #uuid "0b1376ba-3439-5dd7-a72c-5ade3c34e879" :topic/math-academy-id 2281} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139533 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139666 :tutorial/content "The **absolute value** of a number is its *distance from zero* when viewed on a number line.\n\nDistances are never negative. Therefore, the absolute value of a number is always positive or zero.\n\nLet's consider the number $-5$ and its position on a number line, shown below.\n\n![](images/30/3072e47752dc69d38f997e926092f60c24966031d0484241fa9c1133d89d1c56.png)\n\nThe **distance** between $-5$ and zero equals $5$ because $-5$ is five steps away from zero.\n\n![](images/dd/dd155b158a3457eb08a95885461d3ffbd36dd852e1b6c4e9dbda04efed749280.png)\n\nTherefore, *the absolute value of* $-5$ *equals* $5$. We write this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mid \\, {-5} \\, \\mid = 5\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "60694119-254a-584f-b1aa-d0a0d8aeced2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8159 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5023fb7a-0b02-5916-9015-3ae01cfc7dda" :step/math-academy-id 81266 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139534}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139534 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139668 :tutorial/content "There is a fast way to find the absolute value of a number:\n\n- The absolute value of a positive number is equal to itself. For example,\n$\\mid 8 \\mid = 8$.\n- To find the absolute value of a negative number, we drop the negative sign. For instance,\n$\\mid {-8} \\mid = 8$." :tutorial/id #uuid "30b514af-50d6-5cf2-9bec-168ccf25be5e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8367 :tutorial/title "A Fast Way of Finding the Absolute Value"} :step/id #uuid "f16163e2-e8ad-55d9-ab45-85920f3debf6" :step/math-academy-id 81267 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139535}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139535 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138543 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138080 :question/id #uuid "651f6d3a-6def-5064-b5c9-872d02a468fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-13045"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b113dd89-f612-5771-a186-27bfc984c3c0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139146}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Absolute Value of a Positive Number"} :step/id #uuid "439c7674-2599-57c2-ba40-61a36bdde131" :step/math-academy-id 81268 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139536}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139536 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138544 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138081 :question/id #uuid "68b6b6aa-1346-5aaa-a909-fbd43513ba04" :question/math-academy-id "e-13047"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3391981c-d61d-509c-a8f3-db9598494e08" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139148}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Absolute Value of a Negative Number"} :step/id #uuid "1242c5ef-8b03-5424-a7c3-063f61399da6" :step/math-academy-id 81272 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139537}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139537 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138545 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138082 :question/id #uuid "f8cde1b6-791d-5b26-be22-59d4cd59f7bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-13049"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5be86966-3bdb-565f-b0bc-9802ae22e7de" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049429}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139150}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Number Given Its Absolute Value and Position on a Number Line"} :step/id #uuid "16286879-9e35-508e-8e51-962ff4f3364b" :step/math-academy-id 81276}] :activity/title "Absolute Value" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138603}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153002} :activity/id #uuid "26b78e45-1997-563c-b456-d53ae655f749" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049935 :topic/id #uuid "1fcf3c5f-af9e-5e5e-b7aa-ff9b3a312835" :topic/math-academy-id 2558} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153002 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153465 :tutorial/content "The **natural numbers** are the positive whole numbers:\n${\\color{blue}1},\\: {\\color{blue}2},\\: {\\color{blue}3},\\: {\\color{blue}4},\\: {\\color{blue}5},\\ldots$\nWe also refer to them as the **counting numbers**.\n\nThere's some debate among mathematicians about whether zero should be considered a natural number. However, for our purposes, zero is *not* a natural number." :tutorial/id #uuid "ddcec145-4774-553b-836d-76a4a5cd221e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9069 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0fa6983b-4c2c-5789-8e95-585a4e680b9e" :step/math-academy-id 172680 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153003}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152124 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151859 :question/id #uuid "960415f3-fe5d-53ea-a164-5960e800fb3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14642"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0075774-6bd8-54c9-b698-4aacd499fce5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049429}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152871}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Numbers as Natural"} :step/id #uuid "bc685578-51b3-5ffb-b8b8-a5cca744297f" :step/math-academy-id 172681 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153004}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153004 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153466 :tutorial/content "When we extend the natural numbers by joining them with zero and the whole negative numbers, we get the **integers**.\n$\\overbrace{\\ldots, {\\color{red}-5},\\: {\\color{red}-4},\\: {\\color{red}-3},\\: {\\color{red}-2},\\: {\\color{red}-1},\\: {\\color{red}0}, \\: \\!\\underbrace{{\\color{blue}1},\\: {\\color{blue}2}, \\: {\\color{blue}3},\\: {\\color{blue}4},\\: {\\color{blue}5},\\ldots}_{\\large\\textrm{Natural numbers}}}^{\\large\\textrm{Integers}}$\n\nWe can represent the relationship between the natural numbers and integers using an inclusion diagram:\n\n![](images/00/00a2725bbe34eb8bc88f7f6dca6f22e624854a0cb469645eb2f4f9b79a38f846.png)\n\nNotice that the natural numbers are *contained within* the integers. In other words, *every* natural number is also an integer." :tutorial/id #uuid "4109da7f-f9b5-5c94-8215-06f5ea408038" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9070 :tutorial/title "The Integers"} :step/id #uuid "bfd8720b-23e8-55fe-8c02-ac787976007a" :step/math-academy-id 172684 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153005}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153005 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152125 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151860 :question/id #uuid "30b0a464-f903-5ee1-b649-97bfb366a73b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14643"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebf3f930-2768-5399-8470-48ac430065a3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152870}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Numbers as Integers"} :step/id #uuid "34d04639-2557-5679-9ab1-fa22b4733ce3" :step/math-academy-id 172685 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153006}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153006 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153467 :tutorial/content "The **rational numbers** consist of all numbers that can be written as the ratio of two integers. For example:\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}-\\dfrac{1}{2}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{3}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}-\\dfrac{2}{3}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nEvery integer is also a rational number because every integer can be expressed as a ratio of two integers. For example,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{black}\\dfrac{2}{1}}, \\qquad {\\color{red}-9} = {\\color{black}\\dfrac{(-9)}{\\phantom{-}1}}\n$$\n\nLikewise, any mixed number is a rational number because it can be written as an improper fraction. For example,\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nis a rational number because\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{2 \\times 3 + 1}{3} = \\dfrac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the rational numbers to our inclusion diagram.\n\n![](images/12/124ced6b317bdd2df4f967e6ea45e613b89d9fa136acb5edf8f7ed86fa8a4c06.png)\n\nNotice that the integers (and therefore the natural numbers) are contained within the rational numbers." :tutorial/id #uuid "3563ca37-8d82-5742-92b2-bb3918a71477" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9071 :tutorial/title "The Rational Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "cc715ab1-8b8b-555b-bd73-97d8d33107c0" :step/math-academy-id 172688 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153007}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152126 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151861 :question/id #uuid "471f680f-5a51-5dac-aa3c-834610690f0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14644"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7063140-eebe-598c-b530-3c13509d359b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152878}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Numbers as Rational"} :step/id #uuid "ba79c281-b939-528f-8605-0a6cd2aa6abd" :step/math-academy-id 172689 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153008}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153468 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following rational number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13\n$$\n\nWe can find the decimal representation of this rational number by dividing $1$ by $3$ using long division, as follows:\n\n| $\\,\\,0 \\,. 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3$$\\:\\ldots$$3$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$\\,\\ldots$ |\n| --- |\n\nNote that, in this case\n\n- the long division process never ends, and\n- the digit $3$ after the decimal point repeats *forever*.\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13 = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nThis is an example of a **repeating decimal**. A repeating decimal is a decimal where the digits after the decimal point continue forever but in a repeating pattern.\n\nIt's convenient to use the following special notation to indicate decimals that repeat indefinitely.\n\n$$\n0.\\overline{3} = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nSome decimals have two or more digits that repeat indefinitely. For example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.78^(―) &= 0.78787878… \\\\\n0.675^(―) &= 0.675675675…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSome repeating decimals have non-repeating digits before the repeating part. For example:\n\n$$\n0.333\\overline{67} = 0.333\\,67\\,67\\,67\\,67\\ldots\n$$\n\nThe key takeaway for this section is that every repeating decimal is rational! In other words, every repeating decimal can be expressed as a ratio of two integers.\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.3^(―) &= \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n0.78^(―) &= \\frac{26}{33} \\\\\n0.33367^(―) &= \\frac{16517}{49500}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn a future lesson, we'll learn how to express a repeating decimal as a fraction. For now, you can verify these results using a calculator." :tutorial/id #uuid "28ff4afd-e8c7-511c-86a4-14d547e6c6d2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9072 :tutorial/title "Repeating Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "8cd64bc9-e39e-5f5c-9c0d-618b3294cff3" :step/math-academy-id 172692 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153009}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152127 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151862 :question/id #uuid "f0bf7717-e639-5889-92fe-7cdcce737a55" :question/math-academy-id "e-14645"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be826645-6351-54ab-b57d-2c2a83a1b68d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050724}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152676}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rational Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "7c946365-2b4a-5987-84b7-84cce8c8a6f4" :step/math-academy-id 172693}] :activity/title "Natural Numbers, Integers, and Rational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152314}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065089} :activity/id #uuid "7d552a48-87b4-5e70-9852-ea30a169d751" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049948 :topic/id #uuid "208ad900-2568-5e64-a594-cff48ca19048" :topic/math-academy-id 299} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065361 :tutorial/content "Multiplying any number by zero *always* equals zero. Below are some examples.\n\n- $5 \\times 0 = 0$\n- $123\\times 0 = 0$\n- $-314\\,159 \\times 0 = 0$\n- $0\\times 0 = 0$\n\nTo see *why* multiplying any number by zero always equals zero, let's take a look at the multiplication table for $5$, shown below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 \\cdot 4 &= 20 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 3 &= 15 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 2 &= 10 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 1 &= 5 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 0 &= ◻\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is reduced by $5$ each time. Following this pattern, we must have\n\n$$\n\\color{blue}\\square = 0\n$$\n\n.\n\nNote that, since multiplication is commutative, multiplying zero by any number (including itself) equals zero as well. Some examples are shown below.\n\n- $0 \\times 5 = 0$\n- $0 \\times 123 = 0$\n- $0 \\times -314\\,159 = 0$\n- $0 \\times 0 = 0$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7ed778da-8d5a-5363-972b-d9435e22a0e1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3119 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "77ea407f-55c3-5258-ba3f-15c16ab12b9a" :step/math-academy-id 16956 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065090}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065090 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064228 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064086 :question/id #uuid "078b5c64-d765-52da-9d87-56c23618c6a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-4635"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42032d61-c2d5-5154-9a9d-d93e69d1ba53" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064923}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Number by Zero"} :step/id #uuid "32f923a1-966b-5c2d-aa49-a238ba8d46c2" :step/math-academy-id 16957 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065091}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065363 :tutorial/content "Dividing zero by any number (other than zero) always equals zero. For example:\n\n- $0 \\div 2 = 0$\n- $0\\div (-456) = 0$\n\nTo understand why dividing zero by any number (other than zero) always equals zero, think about it this way: if you have zero pizzas, and you want to divide zero pizzas among ten friends, then each friend will get exactly zero pizza." :tutorial/id #uuid "65c0e3ec-7962-5911-a3c9-9de126e87edc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3120 :tutorial/title "Dividing Zero by Another Number"} :step/id #uuid "cb642d1f-f4bb-5b57-be91-7221332ef04b" :step/math-academy-id 16959 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065092}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065092 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064229 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064087 :question/id #uuid "897fedd3-db36-5614-886c-c88fba708692" :question/math-academy-id "e-4637"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2097574-c45c-504d-8ec8-44474880cd21" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064768}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Zero by a Number"} :step/id #uuid "28f3ce5f-977b-550b-9bda-7ae39c91c330" :step/math-academy-id 16960 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065093}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065093 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064230 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064088 :question/id #uuid "800d4367-20e6-55a9-8469-7c3654d512a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4638"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3df16e28-70ed-5dd0-9bf0-d56b38f542eb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064771}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Zero by a Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "a914b898-5054-5bf0-b58e-1526eea6a0fa" :step/math-academy-id 16961 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065094}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065094 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065364 :tutorial/content "Division by zero is **undefined.** No number can be divided by zero, not even zero itself.\n\nTo see why, notice that if we divide $6$ by $3$, we get\n\n$$\n6\\div 3 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n\n$$\n6 = 3\\times 2\n$$\n\nHowever, this argument does not work if we divide by zero. Suppose we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThen, by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nand this relationship cannot be true because any number multiplied by zero is zero! Therefore, $$? is not a number." :tutorial/id #uuid "d8152649-f215-53b2-9811-ce74f0d61dc3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3121 :tutorial/title "Dividing by Zero is Undefined"} :step/id #uuid "42aa28fe-57c7-5ba0-9e1f-9bced6b58754" :step/math-academy-id 16962 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065095}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065095 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064231 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064089 :question/id #uuid "c2eb29fb-9a40-5db8-b377-14c50b87fd86" :question/math-academy-id "e-4639"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c76b2b1b-7536-5b3c-a4c4-6f5e5b72e6ec" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064924}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Number by Zero"} :step/id #uuid "027aea00-6bd5-5235-9135-7e82295e6484" :step/math-academy-id 16963}] :activity/title "Multiplying and Dividing With Zero" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064402}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074736} :activity/id #uuid "5e2897c1-674c-573a-bc21-b2ee8969cb98" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050244 :topic/id #uuid "39037448-ec95-5f7d-a4a1-9f6678563857" :topic/math-academy-id 564} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074736 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075016 :tutorial/content "We can use fraction models to multiply unit fractions by whole numbers.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following product:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 13 \\times 2\n$$\n\nFirst, we represent\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nusing a model:\n\n![](images/f0/f08054646558ab944940f1f8f0d8a1077748f9882039e775c6cbdd4d307db102.png)\n\nMultiplication by a whole number is simply a repeated addition. And, since we need to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{3}\n$$\n\nby ${\\color{blue}2}$, we repeat the fraction model $\\color{blue}2$ times, as shown below.\n\n![](images/31/31fcef6d631262e49fc9c9297fd006f935fd7ee8182b0074d77ae22b626efbbc.png)\n\nThere are\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} \\times {\\color{red}1} = 2\n$$\n\nshaded pieces in total. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2 \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0baa395e-db83-5229-83f7-f40fd99e8a4f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6606 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cf5a7b59-9df0-505a-86e0-c7437dc8b262" :step/math-academy-id 39867 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074737}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074737 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073795 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073417 :question/id #uuid "a3241956-5b5b-5760-9383-d7da13f48b16" :question/math-academy-id "e-10548"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95e51046-fc4c-536b-91eb-82c7c298b7be" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051027}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074414}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Product as a Repeated Addition"} :step/id #uuid "f29f88a5-25e3-54f3-b724-70c3d82de050" :step/math-academy-id 39868 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074738}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074738 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073796 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073418 :question/id #uuid "eea25500-f78e-544f-8bf9-7eaacfac84a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10549"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1e7017e-45df-5adf-9ded-3f815b4f5673" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074410}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Unit Fraction by a Whole Number Using Repeated Addition"} :step/id #uuid "ec8f3fd6-e68a-5a08-909a-7ca8734e46ba" :step/math-academy-id 39869 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074740}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073797 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073419 :question/id #uuid "1c074473-dc1c-56c6-825b-be4b6b3dbfa2" :question/math-academy-id "e-10550"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23f5f4f6-9d8c-5ab5-ada8-1212832de40f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074416}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Multiplication Problem Given a Model"} :step/id #uuid "6cd88bb7-dfba-55b2-bb7a-0cce2188c27c" :step/math-academy-id 39871 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074741}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074740 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075017 :tutorial/content "There's another way we can use fraction models to multiply unit fractions by whole numbers.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the multiplication problem below:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10} \\times 7\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing a fraction model to represent\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\n![](images/62/62b50e658ed9bc691a9559548d09f87fbd3b33e007e81b5e78a3235ac4bc3280.png)\n\nNow, rather than repeating our model $7$ times (which would take a long time), we can instead multiply the number of shaded pieces by $7$.\n\nOur model has $1$ shaded piece. So, the resulting model must have\n\n$$\n1 \\times 7 = 7\n$$\n\nshaded pieces:\n\n![](images/2a/2a7d54b1d7a6a6013ee64b601d9dc0ba31c8a25dae0f5f69abba4b0fed0e8ee2.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}\\times 7 = \\dfrac{7}{10}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5b799a98-0619-525c-b2da-433704f26bd8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6608 :tutorial/title "A Faster Way of Computing a Product Using Models"} :step/id #uuid "0ff59236-e8f5-575d-bc8b-731fac2b148a" :step/math-academy-id 39884 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074739}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074741 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073798 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073420 :question/id #uuid "3e10607a-5812-50cd-a2d5-4b7c3ebcbde1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10555"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bf2fa4e-c47e-5d89-a4aa-8e255050f792" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074408}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Missing Factor Given a Model"} :step/id #uuid "111068bf-412d-586d-b167-f6557ad36f93" :step/math-academy-id 39885}] :activity/title "Multiplying Unit Fractions by Whole Numbers Using Models" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073982}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074871} :activity/id #uuid "6d7f90a6-7b6b-52db-bbf3-10269a41623a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050481 :topic/id #uuid "4d30ea10-ea6d-511a-a7e5-cce5fffa2a64" :topic/math-academy-id 552} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074723 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073786 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073658 :question/id #uuid "b3b354ef-26fc-55dc-acdd-4b28c5f273ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-8139"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6a21dbe-0674-5fa2-b3ee-e4069115c6be" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050924}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074383}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Ratio as a Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "1c6ef1b8-edb3-5545-930a-73cc7c32d2b9" :step/math-academy-id 30450 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074872}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074871 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074966 :tutorial/content "We can write ratios as fractions by placing the first number over the second number.\n\nSo, what ratio describes the number of rectangles to triangles below?\n\n![](images/25/25426db008d4b246f36959f51433e0a5cebe609fc01f9539d4f45af353919261.png)\n\nFirst, we count the number of rectangles. We see that there are $\\color{blue}4$ rectangles.\n\nThen, we count the number of triangles. We see that there are $\\color{red}3$ triangles.\n\nSo the ratio of rectangles to triangles is ${\\color{blue}4}:{\\color{red}3}$. As a fraction, this is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nWe interpret this number to mean that there are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nrectangles for each triangle." :tutorial/id #uuid "d0b88e38-94c5-52f0-8b5d-de261fe7f82e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2047 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "710f2d4c-d273-5b72-9ccc-f38b8242da5e" :step/math-academy-id 7777 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074723}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074872 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073879 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073558 :question/id #uuid "44e1e2da-6e2f-50b1-b982-dd5d4b5a432c" :question/math-academy-id "e-2265"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e191386-9391-5e3c-9f2f-f17d55f9b6ca" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050078}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074427}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Statement Describing a Ratio as a Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "ac944726-318f-5b1c-a830-c224ef6cc5c8" :step/math-academy-id 7778 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074873}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074873 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073880 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073559 :question/id #uuid "cd725418-654f-5cb2-a77a-9f6f9ed27825" :question/math-academy-id "e-2267"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7435b531-e922-5db5-8140-463b7e66dd64" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074425}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Statement by Interpreting a Fraction as a Ratio"} :step/id #uuid "94e66fd6-c348-57bd-ad13-f242226deafe" :step/math-academy-id 7780}] :activity/title "Writing Ratios Using Fractions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073971}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055836} :activity/id #uuid "c871a154-cf68-51db-83a7-946dca52e14d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050517 :topic/id #uuid "50123c1c-7113-541e-8c4f-047f2eec416d" :topic/math-academy-id 92} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055836 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055942 :tutorial/content "The **Cartesian coordinate system** (or **Cartesian plane**) is a grid that we can use to understand where certain objects lie, similar to a map. This grid looks as follows:\n\n![](images/fd/fd702a53ff1127a2432cbdd9a3c7057a08c73ac7a086454c066fa2c96bea7974.png)\n\nIt consists of a horizontal number line called the **$x$-axis** and a vertical number line called the **$y$-axis**.\n\nThe **origin** is the location where the $x$ and $y$ axes meet and is given the symbol $O$." :tutorial/id #uuid "dacc8373-6186-517b-bba8-ec1729732e4d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1431 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5cca38c5-c625-5a28-92d3-ccdcc09f108d" :step/math-academy-id 607419 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055837}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055837 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055943 :tutorial/content "Every location on the Cartesian plane is called a **point**. Every point is identified using an $x$**-coordinate** and a $y$**-coordinate**. Together, points are expressed using an **ordered pair** of **coordinates** $(x,y)$.\n\nFor example, we can mark (or **plot**) the point $A$ with coordinates $({\\color{red}{3}},{\\color{blue}{2}})$ by starting from $O$ and then traveling $\\color{red}3$ units towards the right, followed $\\color{blue}2$ units up.\n\n![](images/c1/c1ef61dedbbcc6eedcd125c2c62cdfbaa4d9b9a12f10181714b51c060a059fd3.png)\n\nWe say that\n\n- the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $3$,\n- the $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $2$,\n- the $xy$-coordinates (or just \"the coordinates\") of $A$ are $(3,2)$.\n\nThe letter $A$ is just a label for our point. It's usually a good idea to label points!" :tutorial/id #uuid "9e7847c5-757a-5cb8-b947-9c7d806b5dc5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1432 :tutorial/title "Coordinates and Ordered Pairs"} :step/id #uuid "b81ec1ae-2147-51bf-abd0-42837ec9335a" :step/math-academy-id 607420 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055838}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055838 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055957 :tutorial/content "Remember that a coordinate plane is made by two number lines that meet at $0$. Since these are number lines, we can extend both to include negative numbers.\n\n![](images/f1/f1eeb7f3bc0ab8b39e9dc7127d76a32f1dd130d380748fedc6e11b967f3878ed.png)\n\nWe plot an ordered pair $({\\color{red}x},{\\color{blue}y})$ similar to before, but if the number is negative, we reverse the direction. So, starting at the origin:\n\n- if the first number is positive, move that number of places *right* along the $\\color{red}x$-axis. If its negative, move *left*.\n- if the second number is positive, move that number of places *up* along the $\\color{blue}y$-axis. If its negative, move *down*.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the coordinates of the point $Q$ above.\n\nTo find the coordinates of $Q$, we start at the origin, move $\\color{red}3$ units *left*, and then $\\color{blue}2$ units *down*.\n\n![](images/b4/b4f58f0c1ad6dc2936ed1b24c844fa82c1b1d5ec10c80e14c6a43f6f4eaf5544.png)\n\nSince we move left, the $\\color{red}x$-coordinate is negative, and since we move down, the $\\color{blue}y$-coordinate is negative.\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $Q$ are $({\\color{red}{-3}},{\\color{blue}{-2}})$." :tutorial/id #uuid "7383452c-9470-5f05-b206-dffbb84808dd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2092 :tutorial/title "Extending the Coordinate Plane to Negative Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "a86683d5-cc60-54e3-a1ca-8ddc210beec5" :step/math-academy-id 607421 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055839}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055839 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054806 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054511 :question/id #uuid "e4658959-f2fb-5bb4-ad05-d75b7c74a5d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-2372"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f41c2f3b-9b55-5f32-bb0a-a8397cdf66ce" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050618}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055439}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the X- or Y-Coordinate of a Point in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "708e4952-07a2-52aa-83ee-e86a33ffcc1d" :step/math-academy-id 607422 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055840}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055840 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054807 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054598 :question/id #uuid "bda7aaff-f03f-53bd-b114-d28a46599641" :question/math-academy-id "e-975"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b509b42a-c38a-5400-8f93-f3e9024b16a2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235487}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Coordinates of a Point"} :step/id #uuid "b4fa400d-ccbf-5b93-8bb2-f08b681dc5d0" :step/math-academy-id 607425 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055841}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055841 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054808 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054597 :question/id #uuid "f610b8b9-8e9e-5a64-bc52-c1f939a0f147" :question/math-academy-id "e-974"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5418e0e-fff7-5d08-b915-6e87be27a132" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050618}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055436}] :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Point in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "b5dc0ae8-3ca8-53a2-a100-962fd53b4781" :step/math-academy-id 607428 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055842}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055842 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055958 :tutorial/content "The axes split the coordinate plane into four parts, called **quadrants**.\n\n![](images/93/938125e855fcb96f743c01f2a00d289ef13e0601772bab56cf0f255e9adf4dcd.png)\n\nGoing counter-clockwise, it's common to call\n\n- the top-right quadrant the first quadrant (I),\n- the top-left is the second quadrant (II),\n- the bottom-left is the third quadrant (III),\n- the and bottom-right is the fourth quadrant (IV).\n\nTo remember the order of the quadrants, it's helpful to think about writing the letter \"C\" in the graph:\n\n![](images/1e/1ef91f2bc25ea8b887ec449d2dafe2eaf6a157733e7bc50e4480280df8190728.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "8a1e27dd-9f87-5adf-b0c6-f0f6cd7e2aac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2097 :tutorial/title "Quadrants in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "3caa8c6c-0602-56f3-a037-3db5c98ff22b" :step/math-academy-id 607431 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055843}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055843 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054809 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054599 :question/id #uuid "419738bb-56e5-52db-aa34-42ccd945fe64" :question/math-academy-id "e-977"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7fcec0d8-ca03-5446-a42f-65d0ccddd584" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055437}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Quadrant of a Given Point"} :step/id #uuid "e7a9127a-071f-5089-a135-f41568b6862d" :step/math-academy-id 607432}] :activity/title "The Cartesian Coordinate System" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054933}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143632} :activity/id #uuid "78db3c1b-5958-5d06-912e-feba2d0d67f3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050599 :topic/id #uuid "576878c2-12a4-51b4-9889-1011d89192c4" :topic/math-academy-id 2360} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144029 :tutorial/content "We can use models to multiply fractions by whole numbers. But can we do this without using models?\n\nAs it turns out, models give us vital clues on how this should be done!\n\nTo demonstrate, let's take a look at the fraction model for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 9 \\times 3\n$$\n\n![](images/47/4700c7ddbdc849a73d69ba41d2d716218f1fb181e8e5e9b8757392db6d9a6243.png)\n\nIn the answer, we end up with\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{2}}\\times {\\color{red}{3}} = 6\n$$\n\nparts, and the denominator remains the same. This is always the case.\n\nTherefore, to multiply a fraction by a whole number, we follow the following steps:\n\n> **Step 1**: We multiply the numerator by the whole number.\n> **Step 2**: We keep the denominator the same.\n\nUsing these steps, we can figure out the answer without using models:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{9}{\\color{red}\\,\\times 3\\,} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{2}}{\\color{red}\\,\\times 3\\,}}{9} = \\dfrac{6}{9}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e1ad38dc-1875-52f8-ad8b-fb013451e541" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8393 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ec9e5f6f-5761-5bfe-a807-c02ebc68c07b" :step/math-academy-id 130656 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143633}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142772 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142510 :question/id #uuid "714ed22d-8268-502d-9d74-12620cb1c4fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-13393"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c58126c-3d5b-5c2e-aaa4-7f9304e77935" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050244}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143287}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Fraction by a Whole Number"} :step/id #uuid "0650c7dd-3214-5c51-8afb-c59493c3db45" :step/math-academy-id 130657 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143634}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142773 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142511 :question/id #uuid "499b112b-863b-568a-bd56-f32d66b619fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-13394"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3c96e1c-a8cd-5d6d-b4ae-7497c4694407" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143267}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Fraction by a Whole Number Resulting in a Whole Number"} :step/id #uuid "4e5e79ca-ec3a-5392-9974-2808857187dd" :step/math-academy-id 130661 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143635}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142774 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142512 :question/id #uuid "da8475a1-d48e-5989-b1a9-a7c72a205fd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-13395"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3bbd0c1-1e52-5f95-b5b1-7270f49e5f5d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050078}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143268}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Fraction by a Whole Number With Simplification"} :step/id #uuid "21db46b2-cb05-59f7-946c-993b805b99a2" :step/math-academy-id 130665 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142775 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142513 :question/id #uuid "494ad564-7a01-533e-887a-6c83a2fb4e78" :question/math-academy-id "e-13396"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91037b32-189a-5ba9-ab0a-117f6255c496" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050249}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143197}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Fraction by a Whole Number Resulting in a Mixed Number"} :step/id #uuid "130fe104-c8f0-562f-9d3d-edb7b1985250" :step/math-academy-id 130669}] :activity/title "Multiplying Fractions by Whole Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186142997}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055825} :activity/id #uuid "604975a3-93f4-5972-a157-950937a51eea" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050618 :topic/id #uuid "598e4586-378e-5300-9abe-bbb9ded2ad77" :topic/math-academy-id 63} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055825 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056032 :tutorial/content "A **variable** is any letter or symbol that can be replaced by a number. For example, the letters\n$x, \\quad y, \\quad z, \\quad a, \\quad b, \\quad c, \\quad k, \\quad m,\\quad n,\\quad p, \\quad q,\\quad r, \\quad \\ldots$\n\nare commonly used as variables. But in general, we can use any letter we like.\n\nAn **algebraic expression** is a combination of variables with mathematical operations, like addition $(+)$, subtraction $(-)$, multiplication $(\\times)$, and division $(\\div)$. So\n$x+2,\\qquad 6-y,\\qquad 2\\times p + 1,\\qquad q\\div r$,\nare all examples of algebraic expressions." :tutorial/id #uuid "232cd5fe-049f-512d-8d7c-ad600a7e6a81" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8165 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "56c96a42-70b1-5c3d-baeb-d0e31a2f412b" :step/math-academy-id 53778 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055826}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055826 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054799 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054421 :question/id #uuid "61ebe3c8-1241-59f1-9d60-704d520c53fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-13055"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef650886-8d46-583e-bad0-1429ba496479" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055390}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Variables"} :step/id #uuid "31658058-879c-5f54-87bd-336129b73d45" :step/math-academy-id 53779 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055827}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055827 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054800 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054422 :question/id #uuid "ee12fc6a-91b2-5855-96a8-c28bd6652ae5" :question/math-academy-id "e-13056"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae662093-c3e8-5577-af6e-7e2f383da79d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055391}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating Between Phrases and Algebraic Expressions Containing Addition"} :step/id #uuid "0db792bd-948d-59a9-bc0b-02a8fbacf35a" :step/math-academy-id 53782 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055828}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055828 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054801 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054423 :question/id #uuid "ebd7ac34-c0e4-5e42-940a-cce33864a7de" :question/math-academy-id "e-13057"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fc32821-14d0-5cbe-b8d9-4e7ff5cc937b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055392}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating Between Phrases and Algebraic Expressions Containing Subtraction"} :step/id #uuid "efce8a57-ebe0-5019-8c9d-0c9aa1ff8814" :step/math-academy-id 53786}] :activity/title "Introduction to Algebraic Expressions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054915}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074610} :activity/id #uuid "d1e56cd5-fc3b-5ec2-ae90-20ca4a2e732f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050665 :topic/id #uuid "5d634c2c-a4e7-591a-891e-89c65f30d787" :topic/math-academy-id 529} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074610 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075043 :tutorial/content "Remember that a **unit fraction** is a fraction containing $1$ in the numerator.\n\nFor example, the following fractions are unit fractions since they each contain ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{2}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{5}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{8}\n$$\n\nHowever, the following fractions are *not* unit fractions since they do *not* contain ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}{2}}{3}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}{5}}{8}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}{7}}{12}\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we will use models to divide whole numbers by unit fractions.\n\nLet's start with an example. Suppose we divide the pizza below into fifths. How many fifths will there be in total?\n\n![](images/4a/4a73488a784067fbe0a4281ef8de45209e284bcd8675f5476c14a8bdcb357712.png)\n\nSince each part is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nof a whole, we need to split $\\color{red}1$ pizza (whole) into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9e36c7b607bd6ada0018e574d52c060851837b62025429b6cfb94f98ed378ccd.png)\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}1} \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 5\n$$\n\nparts in total." :tutorial/id #uuid "577d7682-3ed8-5103-8641-0c02702a9e08" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8557 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "26245a65-ebc8-5e42-9300-6d874c79a4ec" :step/math-academy-id 172318 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074611}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073720 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073461 :question/id #uuid "4f8f2348-c6ec-5073-9f95-6697173654a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13690"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2b57f76-8fab-5b44-80ee-d905e5f9a14f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049517}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074521}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining How Many Parts Fit Into a Whole"} :step/id #uuid "3c05bf1b-ab93-581a-9e20-32a418b2cda1" :step/math-academy-id 172319 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074612}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075044 :tutorial/content "The model below represents $2$ wholes. Let's use it to calculate the value of\n\n$$\n2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\n![](images/bc/bceafee7d9ca5e92e69b5d33d9504f4290526b80059ad62ed26229d02712a278.png)\n\nTo calculate\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2} \\div \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\n, we need to divide each of $\\color{red}2$ wholes into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cf/cf608ce8f5d8e2a47a4bebe21f198d193b10dd8f6e95436986bc954c89c60d19.png)\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2} \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 10\n$$\n\npieces in total.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{5} = 10\\,\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2de869cb-775d-5db4-b90d-7df0de218fc0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8561 :tutorial/title "Dividing Whole Numbers by Unit Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "480191b5-d5a1-50b4-8e0c-c36a3b10da79" :step/math-academy-id 172323 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073721 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073462 :question/id #uuid "2f739b94-f494-546b-8623-bd17a525a436" :question/math-academy-id "e-13695"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed8053e5-f273-56f2-bfb6-294f936d9701" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074386}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Model Representing a Division Problem"} :step/id #uuid "242be67e-027e-54a4-9866-c63cb081faa9" :step/math-academy-id 172324 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074614}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073722 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073467 :question/id #uuid "dd5b4bc8-110c-5247-b5e6-ee4ce7636a1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13707"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e33ab8e-736b-5972-af0d-f7eeabda1148" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074366}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Division Problem Represented by a Model"} :step/id #uuid "7721ecc8-af47-5723-b955-d68c3dc4eecb" :step/math-academy-id 172327 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074615}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073723 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073468 :question/id #uuid "06e730e3-2df3-5d28-afd1-72c34d11cf8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13709"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79e310e4-b8a2-5327-b4e6-fd71b6783e3a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074387}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Whole Numbers by Unit Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "b662cd66-040d-56ae-be80-ddf72c03e975" :step/math-academy-id 172330}] :activity/title "Dividing Whole Numbers by Unit Fractions Using Models" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073949}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148258} :activity/id #uuid "df30f4e3-c67c-5307-817c-9487c3f27d8d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050703 :topic/id #uuid "60339f28-f134-5b2c-b1db-ead03d862fa1" :topic/math-academy-id 2477} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148258 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148798 :tutorial/content "We can use our knowledge of decimal multiplication to evaluate decimal exponents.\n\nTo illustrate, let's evaluate $(0.4)^2$.\n\nFirst, by writing the expression with multiplication signs, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(0.4)^{2} &= 0.4 \\cdot 0.4_(⏟)_([math]{\\color{blue}{2}}[/math] times)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute this product, we first ignore any decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers. We also ignore any leading zeros during the multiplication. This gives\n\n$$\n2\n$$\n\nWe now count the number of decimal places in the $\\color{blue}2$ original factors.\n\nThere is ${\\color{purple}{1}}$ decimal place in $0.4$.\nTherefore, the exponent $(0.4)^{\\color{blue}2}$ will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}{1}}\\times {\\color{blue}{2}} = \\color{red}2\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $16$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $\\color{red}2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(0.4)^2 = 0.16\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d99a8d89-2de3-5681-a3e1-6a6aa68ab428" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8957 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e48ecd14-a46c-5650-ad56-0f866f37721c" :step/math-academy-id 151912 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148259}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148259 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147412 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147183 :question/id #uuid "027603b5-e3f3-55a1-836b-d41fb555bfec" :question/math-academy-id "e-14479"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc0f11b9-07e3-5659-ab34-4ad1c30310d9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049368}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147940}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating One-Digit Decimal Exponents"} :step/id #uuid "c9e8d78d-6f3f-52af-9821-dd755a51560d" :step/math-academy-id 151913 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148260}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148260 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147413 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147184 :question/id #uuid "597f5dfa-9e92-58c0-a3dd-f8205a382dc2" :question/math-academy-id "e-14480"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7701af1-73f4-5da0-a029-1737dfbec48d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147941}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Two-Digit Decimal Exponents"} :step/id #uuid "3d4fc228-ca4d-50f3-8d5b-ccf710a05837" :step/math-academy-id 151917 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148261}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148261 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147414 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147185 :question/id #uuid "e798b10f-9001-5a30-a696-73523bc629bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-14482"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04101ec9-db1c-5618-a272-fb94e9b4c188" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049057}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147947}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Three-Digit Decimal Exponents"} :step/id #uuid "b18f6f42-9f33-5dcc-b9b0-bc6413ee5bb9" :step/math-academy-id 151921}] :activity/title "Evaluating Exponents With Decimal Bases" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147677}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134596} :activity/id #uuid "682c47c0-744b-5245-9512-733920b3dc7e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050838 :topic/id #uuid "6a8e722d-b3fa-5144-a35c-61c8756571f7" :topic/math-academy-id 2195} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135183 :tutorial/content "To **cube** a number means to multiply that number by itself and then multiply the result by the original number once more.\n\nFor example, to \"cube $2$\", we need to multiply $2$ by $2$ to make $4$, and then multiply this result by $2$ once more. We write the \"cube of $2$\" as $2^3$, so we have\n\n$$\n2^3 = \\underbrace{2\\cdot 2}_{\\large 4} \\cdot 2 = 4 \\cdot 2 = 8\n$$\n\nWe would then say that \"the cube of $2$ is $8$\". We could also say \"$2$ cubed equals $8$\".\n\nThe number ${\\color{blue}3}$ in the expression $2^{\\color{blue}3}$ is called the power (or exponent).\n\nThe act of cubing a number can be represented geometrically. For example, let's start by representing the number $2$ with the following collection of two items:\n\n![](images/b6/b6a13017dcbcf25a66ab576189690e8879a4ccbb3c4c5562b129f4dd15af4e85.png)\n\nThen we can picture $2^3$ by creating a bigger shape (called a **cube**) with length, width and height all equal to $2$:\n\n![](images/30/305e02bdaa4c3efcb9d8047eeb3d3573f39c08714e30ba6053cfc4441053156b.png)\n\nThere are $8$ items in total in our big cube (one little cube is hiding behind the others), indicating that $2^3=8$.\n\nClearly,\n\n$$\n1^3=1\\cdot1\\cdot1={\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\nThe next few first basic cubes are shown below:\n\n| $2^3=2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2={\\color{blue}8}$ | $3^3=3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3={\\color{blue}27}$ | $4^3=4 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 4={\\color{blue}64}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| ![](images/30/305e02bdaa4c3efcb9d8047eeb3d3573f39c08714e30ba6053cfc4441053156b.png) | ![](images/cb/cb3171dc7f7a2e9838c3e8b46c3ce385be28624b4474391b9bb211b5a599fa55.png) | ![](images/65/6546ffc5df407b29df601252e1f5afa8e01b17dca5c7169a6321d550d86255c3.png) |\n\nThe cubes of our usual counting numbers are called **cube numbers** or **perfect cubes**. We can write them as follows:\n$1^3, 2^3, 3^3, 4^3, 5^3, 6^3, \\ldots \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad {\\color{blue}1}, {\\color{blue}8}, {\\color{blue}27}, {\\color{blue}64}, {\\color{blue}125}, {\\color{blue}216}, \\ldots$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1bd95aec-179b-5dc4-b7bd-da02ca5932f2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8272 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9ccabb01-ff56-5686-b294-bd66b4e89e88" :step/math-academy-id 151851 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134597}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134597 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133738 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133511 :question/id #uuid "3e2f3025-cfc0-5688-a5be-dbe7f98848e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13248"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39ed44f2-c5ca-5920-9bb0-4ea26ff3207a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050428}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134570}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cube of a Number"} :step/id #uuid "45f96aa2-301d-5486-9223-105546674dc7" :step/math-academy-id 151852 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134598}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134598 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135184 :tutorial/content "Cubing a negative number *always* results in another negative number.\n\nThis is because a negative times a negative gives a positive value, and then a positive times a negative is a negative again:\n\n$$\n(-)\\cdot(-)\\cdot(-) = (-)\\cdot(-)_(⏟)_((+))\\cdot(-) = (+)\\cdot(-) = (-)\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}-5})^3 = \\underbrace{({\\color{red}-5}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5})}_{\\large {\\color{blue}25}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5}) = {\\color{blue}25} \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5}) = {\\color{red}-125}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e462ead2-4e55-5dac-a940-22768a77546e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8273 :tutorial/title "The Cube of a Negative Number"} :step/id #uuid "fd815b69-da3f-5d4e-b49a-e75f1d8f0398" :step/math-academy-id 151856 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134599}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134599 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133739 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133512 :question/id #uuid "02fa99aa-811e-5d0a-b278-24ec7697a038" :question/math-academy-id "e-13249"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cb81a65-ae88-591a-bb87-0ecb7124b154" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050287}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134412}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cube of a Negative Number"} :step/id #uuid "79b94128-fada-50aa-8ec5-3f3315b85c77" :step/math-academy-id 151857 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134600}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134600 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133740 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133513 :question/id #uuid "5468caae-f88a-59d2-98ab-7941bf7cd81a" :question/math-academy-id "e-13251"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70e7585e-7380-5dbc-9272-b358f085429c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049253}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134383}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cube of a Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "266a9ffe-5ee6-5f21-95a7-a8aea3b7a60d" :step/math-academy-id 151861 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134601}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134601 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133741 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133514 :question/id #uuid "ba7b4d05-42b6-58a5-9607-b30742991005" :question/math-academy-id "e-13253"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09de45b3-1a35-55e3-9886-2e0533f237d6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049194}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134510}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cube of a Decimal"} :step/id #uuid "2fd6a3e8-b2b4-5760-a3c1-067f79a80a5b" :step/math-academy-id 151864}] :activity/title "Cubing Rational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134039}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088734} :activity/id #uuid "577ff41d-cd6b-5bfb-9a19-db4815d18bb2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050915 :topic/id #uuid "70dfa99f-c825-5da5-8721-f8fa26795d6f" :topic/math-academy-id 840} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088734 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088985 :tutorial/content "Remember that a number is a **factor** of another number if it divides the number with no remainder.\n\nA whole number is a **prime number** if it has *exactly two* factors: itself and $1$.\n\nFor instance, $3$ is a prime number because it has only two factors, namely $3$ and $1{:}$\n\n$$\n3 = 3\\times 1\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many prime numbers. The first few prime numbers are\n\n$$\n2,\\quad 3,\\quad 5,\\quad 7,\\quad 11,\\quad 13,\\quad 17,\\quad 19,\\quad 23,\\quad 29,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nPrime numbers are very important. For example, computers use them to send and receive information across the internet securely." :tutorial/id #uuid "ee084b63-b5c9-54fe-8f73-750445535efe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6639 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "61039992-084b-5a41-8a84-d5d32f71239e" :step/math-academy-id 40187 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088736}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088735 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088986 :tutorial/content "Numbers with *more* than two factors are called **composite numbers**.\n\nFor example, $4$ is composite because it has three factors, namely $1, 2$, and $4$.\n\nWe can think of composite numbers geometrically. Let the number $4$ be represented by a collection of four items:\n\n![](images/5b/5b0d7581cb6e9ce19bd9337ee1311ceb5260943b96ecd50984db3b3a03a5dd87.png)\n\nWe can pile the objects up to form a rectangle where the length and width are both greater than $1{:}$\n\n![](images/d6/d6d1f8ff0c888300090d1f3c438abcaed9f30e898f4cf7b5b2d6ccbbad27e831.png)\n\nIn contrast, a prime number of objects *cannot* be rearranged to form a rectangle where the length and width are *both* greater than $1$." :tutorial/id #uuid "db98b259-0f36-53b5-b62a-788af51d2b32" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6640 :tutorial/title "Composite Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "dbf16eb4-9e74-5637-b88b-0c59bf07c9e3" :step/math-academy-id 40188 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088737}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088736 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087825 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087420 :question/id #uuid "580e631f-10aa-5f3c-92a9-592c879df478" :question/math-academy-id "e-10623"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af9aa6af-5cbe-5933-9c7b-62e32a63386c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048351}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088011}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Prime Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "4c44f455-2994-5b1f-b5a9-5ea9a33d7a2a" :step/math-academy-id 40189 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088735}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088737 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087826 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087421 :question/id #uuid "f1e2d3d3-8819-5988-8ec1-195cbd548773" :question/math-academy-id "e-10624"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd997467-85ee-5288-a574-2719cbff167b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088015}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Composite Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "b769077a-6aa9-51b2-b700-b41bc8260115" :step/math-academy-id 40192 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088739}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088738 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087827 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087422 :question/id #uuid "72d7834c-fdfd-5f05-ad5f-cd9f6a2fcffc" :question/math-academy-id "e-10625"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b45eb1fc-7e00-5ece-8dcf-f27e6d4fb78e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088013}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying One as Neither Prime Nor Composite"} :step/id #uuid "03211f8f-c13f-570c-b8d1-d99bafd6d977" :step/math-academy-id 40196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088987 :tutorial/content "Every positive whole number is either prime or composite, but not both.\n\nThe only exception is the number $1$. The number $1$ is neither prime nor composite because it only has one whole number factor, namely $1$." :tutorial/id #uuid "2092df40-d7b5-5553-a2d1-05d8166f06bb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6646 :tutorial/title "One Is Neither Prime Nor Composite"} :step/id #uuid "758bd934-4f53-5080-bca6-e617305ccced" :step/math-academy-id 40237 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088738}}] :activity/title "Prime and Composite Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087912}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074630} :activity/id #uuid "8d68098e-61ea-5694-8c81-2a07b3b89448" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050924 :topic/id #uuid "7247d928-5458-5a46-a08c-b035b4eadd8a" :topic/math-academy-id 534} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075037 :tutorial/content "A **ratio** is a mathematical statement that expresses the relative sizes of two quantities.\n\nAs an example, let's look at the triangles and circles below.\n\n![](images/48/4865786ce55e0ec3fc1747ece4ea962425a3f83b2a45c7980f52719522318c82.png)\n\nWe can express the ratio of the number of circles to the number of triangles as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\textrm{number of circles}}}: {\\color{red}{\\textrm{number of triangles}}}\n$$\n\nTo find this ratio, we start by counting the number of occurrences of each shape:\n\n- First, we count the number of circles. There are $\\color{blue}5$ circles.\n- Next, we count the number of triangles. There are $\\color{red}2$ triangles.\n\nTherefore, the ratio of circles to triangles is ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}2}$.\n\nWe can describe this ratio in words as follows:\n\n> *The ratio of circles to triangles is ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}2}$ because there are ${\\color{blue}5}$ circles*for every*${\\color{red}{2}}$ triangles.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "3d8ebe22-8ef7-538a-a42d-4f1133689833" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8277 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0340ef01-84ac-534c-a79b-d7336d5305b6" :step/math-academy-id 185365 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074631}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074631 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073735 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073450 :question/id #uuid "c4235bf2-0c2b-5c2d-be8d-779a8e5fb38f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13256"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0be25cd8-4333-5e00-91e9-7e1256281814" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048698}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074396}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Ratio"} :step/id #uuid "310791d0-15c7-54f2-9d96-846dc8064a94" :step/math-academy-id 185366 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074632}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073736 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073451 :question/id #uuid "76442c46-6958-556f-a895-027a3ce7427b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13257"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "acde6034-9007-5777-a79c-5f99ead9154a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074401}] :knowledge-point/title "Word Problems"} :step/id #uuid "cb26c220-5990-5446-ae5f-4ca0b272e25a" :step/math-academy-id 185369 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074633}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073737 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073452 :question/id #uuid "34ec50f1-1873-510d-8693-49f93bae1df8" :question/math-academy-id "e-13258"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9edb7c90-39fa-5396-b00e-04a0627d0581" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074402}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Ratios"} :step/id #uuid "9ef29b30-f1cb-5cec-a28d-cd61e7444890" :step/math-academy-id 185372}] :activity/title "Introduction to Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073954}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148246} :activity/id #uuid "4b9a6864-adeb-55b6-a307-9ae8a7bb1b59" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050927 :topic/id #uuid "7275db81-0f7a-5732-8201-28cd81b685b9" :topic/math-academy-id 2459} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148246 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148791 :tutorial/content "Any multiplication of fractions can also be interpreted as multiplication and division of whole numbers.\n\nFor example, consider\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{14}{\\color{blue}3} \\times \\dfrac{6}{\\color{red}7}\n$$\n\nWriting the fractions as division, our expression becomes\n$(14\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 3}})\\times (6\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}})$.\nWe use parentheses to make it easier to read.\n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can write an equivalent expression by swapping the two divisions. Swapping the ${\\color{red}{\\div\\, 3}}$ and the ${\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 7}}$, we get the following equivalent expression:\n$(14\\, {\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}}) \\times (6\\, {\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 3}})$\n\nIf we interpret the divisions as fractions again, then we have another equivalent expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{14}{\\color{red}7} \\times \\dfrac{6}{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nNotice that this amounts to *swapping the denominators* in the original expression." :tutorial/id #uuid "c473aa48-4570-5eb6-9482-3e3e9c088ea6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8589 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "eb2cdf86-6a92-5c47-ad2b-84d5074cc074" :step/math-academy-id 135500 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148247}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148247 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147404 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147168 :question/id #uuid "ba1bbec5-0ff2-52ec-ac2e-876a8ea4c70e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13755"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e0f80f6-920a-5eb0-919c-62c8ba67a2b9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050475}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147901}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Multiplication and Division as Fraction Multiplication"} :step/id #uuid "ac10ae6e-8027-5c0a-a3ee-255e0e11895c" :step/math-academy-id 135501 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148248}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148248 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147405 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147169 :question/id #uuid "ebdd2229-6712-561c-821b-7286460a6824" :question/math-academy-id "e-13758"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2d79438-2593-55c1-a937-c40f8fc6db08" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147902}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Expression as an Equivalent Product of Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "2bc90864-268c-52ba-b368-f50e8a0eee6d" :step/math-academy-id 135504 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148249}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148249 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147406 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147170 :question/id #uuid "830169cc-f331-5a91-81d6-374aad29e951" :question/math-academy-id "e-13761"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8b307c7-79bc-58de-ab88-b0783857371b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147903}] :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Answer Is a Whole Number"} :step/id #uuid "5d27849a-7867-5a73-8f8d-1b379b861f6b" :step/math-academy-id 135507 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148250}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148792 :tutorial/content "When solving a fraction multiplication problem, it's often convenient to simplify the problem *before* carrying out the multiplication.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{8} \\times \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}12}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's numerator ($\\color{red}3$) and the second fraction's denominator ($\\color{blue}12$) have a common\nfactor of $3$.\n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by first swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}12} \\times \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the first fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3 \\div 3}{12 \\div 3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{8} &= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\times \\dfrac{1}{8} = \\dfrac{1 \\times 1}{4 \\times 8} = \\dfrac{1}{32}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{8} \\times \\dfrac{1}{12} = \\dfrac{1}{32}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c36b33ff-a822-5308-95a8-990652f0aab8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8591 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Products of Fractions Using a Strategy"} :step/id #uuid "e26ba33d-782f-55f9-9be0-d0fb0d88f75b" :step/math-academy-id 135510 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148251}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148251 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147407 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147186 :question/id #uuid "36b559ef-d696-580d-813f-c80fc9bc36b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-14704"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee7ebc5-72be-53b1-86d8-27bd939a7244" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049253}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147743}] :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Answer Is a Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "a8d8bfe8-541a-557f-9e2b-0145547a3650" :step/math-academy-id 135511}] :activity/title "Multiplying Fractions by Swapping Denominators" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147660}]}
